WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.400
Male northern elephant seals

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.200
know who their rivals are on the beach.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.600
They don’t even have to see the other guy.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.460
They remember his voice

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.460
and the rhythm in his call.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.320
Humans do the same thing

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.320
when we listen to other people's voices.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.000
That’s how we can put a name to a voice,

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.620
even without seeing someone

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.440
For elephant seals,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.900
learning and remembering other males’ calls

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.780
is a matter of survival.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.640
Every winter, the males gather at rookeries

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.400
from Northern California to Baja Mexico,

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:45.300
to fight and mate.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.000
They fight for females--

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.520
raising their front flippers and rhythmically roaring.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:57.000
By playing back natural and modified calls

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.400
to a seal colony,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.780
scientists showed that

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.180
males remember each other’s voices.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.280
When subordinate males heard

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.080
an alpha’s call that was sped-up or slowed down,

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.640
they stayed put.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:11.700
But they fled

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.940
if the call was similar to the alpha’s normal one.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:19.840
Subordinate males are usually smaller than the alphas,

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.660
and they move away

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.680
when they hear a dominant male call.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.160
Males that don’t move away are attacked.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.140
Alphas violently lunge at competitors,

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.580
sometimes causing serious—even lethal—injuries.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.440
So it makes sense for the male elephant seals

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.440
to memorize their rivals’ voices.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.720
They need to use those sounds

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.260
to decide on the best strategy:

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:44.460
flee or fight.

